 1. INTRODUCTION

CPU scheduling is an important concept in operating systems that determines how processes are executed by the CPU.

The Round Robin scheduling algorithm is one of the simplest and most widely used CPU scheduling techniques. It assigns a fixed time quantum to each process in a circular order, ensuring fair CPU allocation among all processes.

This project simulates the Round Robin scheduling algorithm using the C programming language and calculates important performance metrics.


2. OBJECTIVES

The main objectives of this project are:

To implement Round Robin CPU scheduling
To simulate process execution using time quantum
To handle process arrival times and CPU idle conditions
To calculate performance metrics
To display results using Gantt chart visualization


 3. SYSTEM DESIGN (MODULE DESCRIPTION)

The system is divided into four main modules:

3.1 Input Module

Responsible for:

Accepting number of processes
Reading arrival time, burst time, and priority
Validating input values
Storing data in process structures


3.2 Scheduler Module

Responsible for:

Implementing Round Robin algorithm
Managing ready queue
Handling process execution
Tracking CPU idle time
Generating execution order (Gantt chart data)

3.3 Metrics Module

Responsible for calculating:

Turnaround Time (TAT)
Waiting Time (WT)
Response Time (RT)
CPU Utilization
Throughput


3.4 Display Module

Responsible for:

Displaying process table
Printing Gantt chart
Showing performance summary

 4. ALGORITHM (ROUND ROBIN)

The Round Robin algorithm works as follows:

Processes are added to a ready queue based on arrival time
Each process is assigned a fixed time quantum
The CPU executes each process for the given quantum
If a process is not completed, it is placed back into the queue
The process repeats until all processes are completed

 Formulas Used

Turnaround Time (TAT) = Completion Time − Arrival Time
Waiting Time (WT) = Turnaround Time − Burst Time
Response Time (RT) = First Execution − Arrival Time
CPU Utilization = (Busy Time / Total Time) × 100
Throughput = Number of Processes / Total Time

🧪 5. TESTING

The system was tested using different scenarios:


 5.1 Small Input Test
Single process was tested
Verified correct execution without context switching

 5.2 Large Input Test
Multiple processes were tested
Verified fair CPU allocation using Round Robin

5.3 Edge Case Testing
Case 1: CPU Idle Time
CPU remains idle when no process is available
Verified correct idle handling
Case 2: Same Arrival Time
All processes arrive at time 0
Verified fair rotation


 6. OUTPUT ANALYSIS

The system produces:

Process Table (CT, TAT, WT, RT)
Gantt Chart visualization
CPU Utilization percentage
Throughput value
Average performance metrics

All outputs are correctly calculated and validated through test cases.

